WEBVTT

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:12.650
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:21.540
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:31.600
 We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.940
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.680
 You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.140
 And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website, deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:56.720
 Sandor Alex Katz is a fermentation revitalist.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:09.300
 Since publishing Wild Fermentation in 2003, he has taught hundreds of workshops demystifying fermentation and empowering people to reclaim this transformational process in their kitchens.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.040
 The New York Times calls him one of the unlikely rock stars of the American food scene.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:24.250
 Sandor grew up in the pandemic, demanding resources to address the AIDS epidemic.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:30.890
 He migrated from New York City to a commune in rural Tennessee in 1993 after testing positive for HIV.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.270
 He now lives down the road from the commune and travels the world teaching about fermentation.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:46.250
 He talks with Devon about creative tactics and community of ACT UP, the modern relevance of ancient food traditions and migrating from the city to the country.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:54.690
 Sandor, welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.250
 I'm so happy to be talking to you a little bit today.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.470
 Well, thank you so much for having me on your show.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.850
 So, there's a ton of things I want to ask you about.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.990
 Let's talk about where you live.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:08.290
 Yeah, yeah.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:15.070
 I want to hear a little bit about where you live now and what you've got going on and just what the springtime is like there.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:22.580
 Well, I live in Middle Tennessee and we are definitely in high spring.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.820
 The trees are all leafed out.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.460
 The irises are blooming.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:34.220
 We're planting everything in the garden, eating the earliest things out of the garden, lettuce and radishes.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:43.870
 So, yeah, it's high spring down here and I migrated down here 25 years ago from New York City where I grew up.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:51.700
 You know, sometimes the people in New York assume that Tennessee has no seasons and is hot all the time and that's definitely not true.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:03:00.160
 We definitely have four full seasons but thankfully a shorter and milder winter than I grew up with in New York.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.270
 Sandor, when you grew up in New York City, right?

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:05.120
 Yeah.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.240
 What was food like in your world growing up?

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.310
 Well, I mean, we always ate well.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:28.190
 You know, both of my parents liked to cook and we lived in a neighborhood with amazing markets and, you know, lots of vegetables and fruits available and seafood and pickles.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:39.450
 I mean, I definitely grew up loving to eat pickles and I would say my childhood love of pickles is part of what sort of, you know, led me into my fermentation work.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:48.190
 But, you know, we would go out for Chinese food sometimes and sometimes other kinds of restaurants.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:53.970
 But, you know, mostly we would eat at home and, you know, we were always expected to pitch in.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:57.910
 So, you know, we all learned to cook as we were growing up.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.820
 I came across an interview you did for the ACT UP oral history project and I was just reading a little bit about that context.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:10.180
 So, it was kind of, you grew up with a lot of politics in the house too, right?

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:10.920
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:11.320
 Absolutely.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:18.040
 I mean, my family was definitely involved in, you know, various political causes.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:25.120
 And, I mean, I can remember as a little kid going to some marches and demonstrations.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:33.060
 And then when I was a bigger kid, I got pretty involved myself in political campaigns.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:42.120
 And then as I got a little bit older, when I went to college, I got more involved, you know, I would say in some, you know, activist causes.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:52.040
 But, you know, ACT UP, which you're referencing, is certainly the, you know, sort of political cause I got, you know, most directly involved in.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:05:04.860
 Right. Yeah. So, let's talk about ACT UP because it's, I guess, in the bigger sense, I'm interested in talking a lot about radical community and queer community and the way that their work flows through that.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:20.730
 But I'm also interested in this turning point of, you know, working in politics and ACT UP and West Pride and then leaving New York and that kind of politics for rural Tennessee.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:27.980
 So, yeah, ACT UP to me is kind of like there's a legendary moment in New York of organizing around survival.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:35.320
 Yeah, yeah, yeah. No, it was, it definitely was that. It was organizing around survival.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:46.800
 And, you know, we all, myself included, you know, felt, you know, incredibly committed to it because so much was at stake.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.800
 What kinds of organizing were you doing? What were the kinds of tactics and how did those emerge?

00:05:51.080 --> 00:06:04.740
 Well, I mean, you know, there were ACT UP, you know, one thing ACT UP was famous for was these surprise actions that we would call zaps where we would show up, you know, at some, you know, office or some event unannounced.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:20.100
 So there were lots of sort of small, you know, guerrilla actions, but also there were, you know, lots of big actions that were announced and where, you know, the objective was to get as many people there as possible.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:26.080
 So, you know, I definitely attended a lot of demonstrations of both styles.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.600
 One thing I always did in ACT UP was I was a chant leader.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:37.580
 So, you know, just trying to get people riled up and chanting loudly with, you know, relevant topical chants.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:58.510
 You know, we also tried to, you know, especially for the bigger demonstrations, tried to get everybody up to speed and everybody sort of well-versed on the issues so that everybody could be a spokesperson and an articulate spokesperson and, you know, have a real clear sense of the message that we were trying to communicate.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:06.190
 So I was always very involved in, you know, these sort of teach-ins and trainings where we would share information.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:23.400
 And I also always did postering, which would be like going late at night with a bucket of wheat paste and whatever the, you know, poster for the latest event we were publicizing and just walk from where the meetings were up to where I lived on the Upper West Side, putting up posters.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:32.880
 So I was involved in lots of different ways and, you know, it definitely was, you know, an incredible moment.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:39.520
 Friends were dying all the time and that added a huge sense of urgency to it.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.180
 You know, I tested positive myself during that time, which, you know, added to the feeling of urgency.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:08:04.600
 Although in terms of my own migration, I would say that, you know, me testing positive, you know, led me to asking some questions and realizing I needed to make some big changes in my life that ended up resulting in me leaving New York and moving down here to rural Middle Tennessee.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:12.950
 Right. So can you tell me a little bit more about that moment of deciding to leave that one radical community?

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:18.410
 I guess I want to know what that radical community felt like and what made that such a special moment?

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:25.650
 Because it sounds, I mean, it sounds terrifying, but I've heard over and over again that there's something absolutely magical about it, too.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:36.160
 Well, I mean, the times were terrifying because there was disease with no effective treatments that was ravaging our our community.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.000
 And, you know, it was a scary time.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:48.890
 But, you know, I think ACT UP made us feel it made us feel our power.

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:53.410
 It made us feel like, like, OK, we're not powerless in this situation.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:59.510
 You know, this is something that's happening to us, but there are ways that we can respond.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:04.530
 And, you know, clearly this is like a like it's a medical issue.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:10.870
 But to come up with, you know, answers for medical problems takes resources.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:25.270
 And it really took years to get resources devoted to AIDS research, you know, especially when you had political leadership that, you know, like could only laugh like it was a funny joke that this was happening to gay people.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:47.530
 So, I mean, I think that we had this sense of like, you know, kind of righteous indignation and the activism really helped us to, you know, feel our power and find our power to sort of, you know, force the medical research institutions to put resources into finding solutions.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.710
 And, you know, ultimately to, you know, valuing our lives.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.410
 Right.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:54.080
 Right.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:10:01.100
 So, I mean, it wasn't it wasn't sort of, you know, fun to have like, you know, the president chuckling that we were having this problem.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:12.940
 But, you know, I think that it, you know, sort of triggered this sort of, you know, anger that, you know, was transformative, certainly in the lives of the people who were part of it.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:31.790
 And I would say, you know, transformative in the larger arc of the struggle for, you know, rights for LGBT people, you know, and and I think had huge influence over the culture at large.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.560
 Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:41.650
 So, you're doing this activism kind of in solidarity with friends and then you test positive for HIV.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.540
 This is a huge question, but what shifted in you at that point?

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:56.220
 Well, it was huge and it was, you know, it was a shock.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:02.380
 I mean, it's not, you know, I didn't go to get tested for HIV expecting to be positive.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:08.720
 I, you know, I thought that I was being very conscientious and being very, very careful.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.030
 And so, you know, it really shocked me when I tested positive.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:23.450
 And I would say, you know, I walked around for a few months, you know, just kind of like in a state of shock.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:30.100
 Like, you know, I couldn't quite believe that this was the reality of my life that I had to deal with.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:39.110
 And, you know, by that time I had buried a lot of friends, but I also had friends who, you know, had been living for some years with HIV.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:55.030
 So, you know, it's not, I mean, I would say my experience, you know, within H.I., within ACT UP, you know, gave me a lot of role models of people who were not defeatist about it and who were going on living their lives.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:12:05.500
 So, I mean, I'm really thankful for that because, you know, I didn't for a minute, you know, just, you know, think like, oh, this is it.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.680
 Like, you know, I'm gone in a few years.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:11.940
 You know, I should figure out how to plan the end of my life.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.720
 You know, it wasn't a feeling of that at all.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:20.560
 But it did certainly change my ideas about, you know, sort of career and longevity.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:28.800
 And, you know, I pretty much lost interest in my career in municipal government, you know, right away.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:34.180
 And, you know, just started thinking about like, okay, well, what do I really want in life?

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:39.620
 You know, I don't want to be sort of, you know, planning a, you know, 40-year career and my retirement.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:44.580
 I need to, you know, think about like, you know, living in the moment and enjoying life while I've got it.

00:12:46.790 --> 00:13:01.470
 And I would say it was sort of those reflections and, you know, just thinking like, oh, I need to really, you know, make some huge change in my life that, you know, made me open to the idea of moving to Tennessee.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:08.190
 And the way that all came about is that I went to New Orleans for Mardi Gras in 1992.

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:13.510
 My roommates in New York had previously lived in New Orleans and they talked me into coming with them.

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:22.190
 And at New Orleans, I met some of the people, you know, who lived at this rural commune in Tennessee.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:34.890
 And I was very intrigued by them and I was very intrigued by the idea that a rural commune could exist in a place like Tennessee, which I'd never really been to, but, you know, had all kinds of ideas about.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:43.030
 And so I went and I visited and I was very enchanted and I visited again and I felt even more enchanted.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:48.190
 And I started talking to them about, so how does a, how does a person go about moving in here?

00:13:48.190 --> 00:14:05.640
 And then, um, you know, within a month of my, within a year of my first visit, I had, um, sublet my apartment, taken a leave of absence from my job and, um, moved into this, uh, commune in Tennessee, uh, where I stayed and lived for 17 years.

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.740
 Wow. Wow. And, and what were those first few months like living in rural Tennessee?

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.440
 Oh, it was great. Like really from the minute I arrived, I arrived in early March.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:27.040
 So it's still winter in New York, but down here it's like, you know, beautiful springtime and, um, you know, being directly involved in a garden for the first time.

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:35.920
 Um, no, I, I just, I loved it. I loved it. Just spending all my time outdoors and active, um, you know,

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:44.810
 hiking through the woods a lot, learning a lot about the, um, uh, you know, the, the, the plants and wildflowers and herbs of the area.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:58.700
 Um, no, it was, it was, it was, it was a really exciting time in my life. And, um, uh, I never looked back. I really, you know, after a couple of weeks of living down here, I really never thought about moving back to New York.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:09.320
 Um, you know, it just, it just felt so right. And it continues to, I really like, I love, I love living down here. I mean, don't get me wrong. I, I enjoy traveling.

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:18.160
 I, I, I get huge stimulation when I visit New York or, or other big cities. Um, but it's always such a pleasure for me to come home.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:29.090
 Um, and I really, um, you know, I love, uh, living amongst the trees in a lower density place and, um, I have no regrets.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:47.980
 I was just thinking about kind of the food traditions that have come out rural communes in this country, the way that, I don't know, the, um, people on the farm started making tempeh and, um, all these things that are part of our popular food culture have, uh, a lot of them started on, on communes in rural America.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:51.840
 Yeah, let's, let's, let's just understand tempeh did not start on the farm.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:52.820
 Okay, right, right.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:59.240
 Tempeh started in Indonesia. The farm introduced it to American vegetarians, which is different than starting it.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:00.500
 Right, right, right. Okay.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:12.940
 You know, I mean, there was this article in the New York Times a couple of weeks ago that I was quoted in that I found incredibly annoying, but it, you know, it basically was that in the, in the, you know, in the realm of food, the hippies won.

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:15.780
 And she was like, oh yeah, fermentation is a hippie thing.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:24.240
 I was like, what are you talking about? You know, like, okay, you look in the Foxfire books and you can see that the hippies learned it from the old settlers.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:31.260
 And, you know, I mean, it's just like, I mean, sauerkraut is just a practical food if you're preserving that, you know, food without a refrigerator.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:37.260
 Um, so, you know, to, to think that that was a hippie food just seems ridiculous.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:43.800
 And so anyway, I mean, the people at the farm definitely did not invent tempeh and they would be the first ones to tell you that.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:44.860
 Right. Okay.

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:45.800
 So let me rephrase it.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:54.110
 There's, there's this idea that even now in food scholarship and activism, there's this idea that new ideas come from these big cities.

00:16:54.250 --> 00:17:02.620
 When I think about you and fermented foods, and I think about a lot of foods, they don't, they come from rural places, revitalizing them and remixing them.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:07.360
 So they didn't invent tempeh for sure, but there's this revaluing of certain things.

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:13.980
 I pretty much think nobody has invented any new fermented foods or beverages since, let's say, before the time of Jesus.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:29.700
 I'm sure people have clever ideas about, you know, David Chang in New York is, you know, taking these ancient Japanese methods of making miso and, you know, he's applying them to pistachios and to pine nuts.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:41.440
 So, you know, he's applying a traditional process to ingredients that are very different from the ones that the process has always been applied to, but he's not really inventing a new process.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:45.840
 He's just, you know, applying an existing process to a new substrate.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:53.480
 And there's, you know, there's a lot of, you know, really innovative experimentation going on right now and recombination.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:18:15.300
 And I think, you know, because of globalization and really the spread of information more than anything else, you know, people are able to, you know, access information about these rich traditions in one part of the world having to do with one kind of a food and applying them in, you know, new and sometimes very clever ways.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:21.290
 But, you know, they're still working with these ancient processes.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:36.600
 And in terms of like fermenting, you know, I mean, like I've been asked a lot over the last like year or two, you know, to comment on fermentation as, you know, a trend or a fad or something like that.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:49.460
 And I mean, sure, I mean, I mean, I mean, I don't want to deny that there is, you know, growing, you know, like awareness of fermentation, a growing interest in the phenomenon of fermentation.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:57.680
 You know, I'm thrilled that, you know, more and more people in their home kitchens and chefs in their restaurant kitchens are playing around with fermentation.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:01.420
 But there's nothing, you know, new about fermentation.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:14.000
 I mean, you know, I don't think there was ever a time when, you know, bread, cheese, cured meats, condiments, wine, beer, et cetera, waned in popularity.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:24.540
 I mean, you know, certainly in the time when I grew up in the 60s and 70s, certainly when my parents grew up in the 30s and 40s, you know, these foods were just part of everybody's table and everybody's meal.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:26.800
 And maybe no one was talking about them being fermented.

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:36.920
 Well, let's talk about that because that's actually the other migration I wanted to talk to you about is kind of the moving of these ideas and these methods and the knowledge about food around the world.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:41.580
 Actually, my favorite book of yours is The Revolution Will Not Be Microwaved.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:59.710
 And I love the descriptions of, like, of traveling around, doing workshops in these really different kinds of places, like punk houses, fundamentalist Christians, obsessive artisans, and, you know, teaching fermentation, but also, like, getting to know these underground food movements.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:05.850
 People making food that's sometimes illegally, you know, but, I don't know, hidden a little bit from public view.

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.990
 Well, I mean, they're hidden in plain sight.

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:14.750
 I mean, I can tell you my story of how I learned to make sauerkraut for the first time.

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:15.590
 Sure, yeah.

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:22.560
 The joy of cooking, the place where I went to learn how to make everything else that I didn't know how to make that I was trying to figure out.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:31.640
 And, you know, it was just there, you know, it wasn't like it was extremely prominent or that, you know, the book was trying to lead people there.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:36.000
 But there it was in, you know, very simple terms, you know, how to make sauerkraut.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:42.620
 And, you know, I upped my yogurt game from reading The Joy of Cooking also.

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:49.050
 You know, our most basic references have information about fermentation.

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:58.470
 You know, I think, you know, to me, the really interesting migration is about, you know, taking, you know, skills.

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:09.510
 They're, you know, essential cultural information that are key elements of how people make effective use of the food resources that are available to them.

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:26.960
 And, you know, like along with how you grow the foods in the first place, how you save the seeds, you know, how you use fermentation to make use of the food has just been an essential part of what gets passed down from generation to generation.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:33.500
 And if you're going to live, you know, among these people in this area, you're just going to need this information.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:53.320
 And then, you know, over the course of the 20th century, you know, thanks in part to, you know, greater mobility, thanks in part to food mass production, refrigeration, the rise of the supermarket, you know, more and more, you know, convenience foods being available.

00:21:53.680 --> 00:22:01.160
 You know, it was marketed as sort of liberation from the kitchen, liberation from the farm.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:14.020
 But, you know, the flip side of it was, you know, people deciding that it was no longer relevant to them to learn the skills of their grandparents.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:21.340
 And that, you know, they were part of this sort of exciting new world and, you know, they just didn't need to do that kind of labor.

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:22.520
 They had the supermarket.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:35.060
 So, you know, we had sort of a severing in the sort of continuous passing on of these traditions, you know, at the same time as we've had a lot of like, you know, cultural resorting.

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:45.960
 And, you know, most people, you know, would say that they're not the product of like a single cultural tradition, but rather an amalgam of, you know, a number of different cultural traditions.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:51.300
 And, you know, maybe they draw on different ones or maybe they don't really know what they identify with.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:58.920
 But, you know, the world has gotten more complicated and, you know, we have a lot of choices with food.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:03.080
 But at the same time, most of us are fairly disempowered.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:15.700
 So, I mean, I mostly think about fermentation, you know, in terms of, you know, empowerment, you know, that the skills themselves are generally fairly simple and straightforward.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:32.260
 You know, we need to break out of this very confining role of the consumer and, you know, we need to empower ourselves with skills to, you know, take on a more balanced role of being producers as well as consumers.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:36.560
 And I think that fermentation is only one element of that.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:47.500
 But, you know, I think that we need to be reconnecting with the essential cultural information of how to produce food and make effective use of our food resources.

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:55.280
 And that, you know, this sort of system of food mass production and distribution, you know, is really a failed experiment.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:24:01.900
 And, you know, one of our, you know, most critical projects is to reclaim our food.

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.230
 It seems to me like there's a temporary moment in some ways.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:13.470
 Not that that system isn't going strong still, but there's this temporary moment of amnesia about fermented foods and a lot of food craft.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:22.920
 But while that's temporarily forgotten from a bigger culture, there's plenty of people, radicals, of all kinds, keeping up those traditions.

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:30.410
 And it's the moment comes for it to, you know, we just had this drought in California and now it's raining all over again.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:34.030
 And plants that I haven't seen in five years are blooming again.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:39.230
 I mean, I kind of see that that's they've been hanging out somewhere where we haven't seen them.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:44.630
 And that's kind of a lot of the role of these, I don't know, more radical folks hanging on to these traditions and adapting them.

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:49.490
 And then as moments, right, they come out in mass a little bit more.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:52.580
 Sure, sure, sure.

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:59.230
 You wrote this book, The Art of Fermentation, and it has this incredible draw on fermentation traditions from all around the world.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:05.750
 Do you have this library of bubbling things in your garage that represent all these ideas that you've picked up?

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:14.980
 Or do you have stories about some specific or surprising places where you've found fermentation along the way?

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:16.710
 Well, okay.

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:19.750
 So first of all, you start, you asked about a library of bubbling things.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:21.870
 I mean, I have a library of books.

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:31.720
 I mean, you know, an incredible amount of, you know, what I've pieced together has come from books and continues to.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.660
 So sure, I mean, I have experiments now.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:37.780
 So, okay, I tried this experimental thing the other day that I've never done before.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:43.120
 And it's for, it's a Korean style of a starter.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:44.180
 It's called meiju.

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:45.360
 It's made out of soybeans.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:52.600
 And you soak soybeans, cook them until they're soft, mash them up, and then you make like a brick or a ball or a cone.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:56.780
 You make a big shape of the mashed up soybeans.

00:25:56.940 --> 00:26:05.450
 And then you put that in a bed of straw and try to dry it out a little bit and get it to start molding.

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:07.660
 So anyway, you know, I mean, I did this.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:09.680
 It's a pretty simple, straightforward process.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:26.740
 But, you know, I sort of came to this after having followed a recipe by a Korean-American food blogger for making gochujang that I realized at some point after I had made it was fatally flawed in that it had no real starter in it.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:31.760
 And I have this book called Korean Fermentation Technology.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:41.920
 And I found in it a description of this starter called meiju, which was the critical piece that I was missing.

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:50.260
 So anyway, you know, I rely on books and the descriptions that I can find in books of how things are done frequently.

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:52.580
 I mean, I try things, but I can't.

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:58.640
 I mean, like living foods, you can't really maintain as a library unless you're going to keep on making them.

00:26:59.270 --> 00:27:02.630
 So, you know, all of these things that I make have a time frame.

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:07.970
 And that time frame could be measured in hours or days or weeks or months or years.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:12.730
 You know, different projects I've done have different kinds of time frames.

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:20.550
 But I certainly don't attempt to, you know, keep all of these projects going all of the time so I can have a library.

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:26.530
 And, you know, I mean, there are a handful of things that I typically have going all the time.

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:30.890
 You know, I'm always fermenting vegetables, you know, different times of the year.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:34.270
 You might catch me with different styles of fermented vegetables.

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:40.530
 From my recent travels in China, I've been making pao zai, which is the Chinese fermented vegetables.

00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:53.910
 And there's certainly not just one style of it, but I've been experimenting with seasonings and making some sort of seasonings for it that make it, you know, really, really distinctive and different.

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:57.650
 So that's something I've been doing lately.

00:27:57.810 --> 00:28:00.830
 I've been maintaining a sourdough starter for 20 years.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:10.110
 So, you know, sometimes if I travel, I just bury that in the back of my refrigerator and it's fine without ongoing maintenance.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:17.910
 But when I'm at home, I'm, you know, feeding it every couple of days and making pancakes and baking with it and things like that.

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:21.890
 I have a yogurt starter I've been using for years.

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:30.610
 So it's not like I make yogurt every day or every week, but, you know, about once a month I'll make yogurt and, you know, try to make enough to last me.

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:35.010
 So I've got, you know, different projects that are ongoing.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:38.550
 I just hosted a week-long workshop here.

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:41.330
 So, you know, at the moment I have a lot of projects.

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:46.530
 I have a sweet potato fly, which is a lightly fermented soft drink.

00:28:46.930 --> 00:28:48.710
 I have some sake going.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.710
 We just started a batch of miso.

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.190
 So I've got lots going on, actually.

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:55.250
 Let's see.

00:28:55.390 --> 00:29:03.830
 I guess there's a question I want to, I mean, I'm almost embarrassed to ask this because it's like, but it's like, all right, all right.

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:04.650
 So here's it.

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:09.830
 No, I just feel like it's a little trite to ask, like, advice for the times, but I'm going to do it anyway.

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:16.670
 I read that, so you're actually an organizer working against a Trump development in Manhattan at one point, right?

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:18.260
 Right, right, right.

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:18.680
 So, sure.

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:31.480
 30 years ago, I was involved, you know, fighting Donald Trump, trying to stop this, you know, wall of 1170-story buildings that he wanted to build along the Hudson River.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:32.420
 1170s.

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:32.720
 Wow.

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:43.970
 Drawing on, like, we were talking about resistance of ACT UP, and we're drawing on, like, you've been part of a lot of queer community and all this work with fermentation.

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:52.560
 I don't know, what's your take on how we survive this political moment, or how we resist it and find resilience?

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:56.050
 Hmm, yeah.

00:29:56.280 --> 00:30:02.080
 I mean, I wish I had an easy answer to that.

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:13.480
 I mean, I think that, you know, more and more people have to start paying attention, you know, picking up their phones, expressing their outrage to representatives, showing up at demonstrations.

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:23.100
 But, you know, we can't really, you know, like with ACT UP, I mean, the stakes are really, really high.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:47.850
 And, you know, I think that it's really, you know, imperative that, you know, people take the duties of citizenship seriously and, you know, take the time to, you know, express their opinions when they're feeling it, to express their outrage, you know, and definitely to take to the streets and, you know, be part of expressing resistance and making it known.

00:30:47.970 --> 00:30:54.520
 I feel like, I don't know, maybe it's a little early to tell, but I feel like that's happening a lot, or there's a lot of energy behind that.

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.760
 Well, I think you're right.

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:57.900
 I think that it is happening.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:30:59.240
 I mean, I see it.

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:08.720
 I mean, I see it down here in our rural area, you know, the number of people, you know, showing up at the town hall meetings that the legislators have.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:13.480
 You know, the day after the inauguration, I went to this march in Nashville.

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.180
 It was the, you know, the women's march against Trump.

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:28.100
 And, you know, by many orders of magnitude, you know, the largest demonstration I had ever seen there, you know, but I think that, you know, showing up for a single demonstration is not enough.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:33.660
 And, you know, we just have to make this part of our lives in, in an ongoing way.

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:37.370
 And that's what resistance is like.

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:40.590
 It's like, you know, it's, it's an activity, it's a priority.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:58.620
 And I think that, again, to use this phrase, like our responsibility as citizens, you know, I mean, it's not just, it's not just about voting, but it's about, you know, making our feelings known, you know, especially when, you know, the people in power aren't interested in them.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:14.070
 Right. Yeah. I guess the other, the other side of that I want to ask you about is how do we keep that up in community or how do we stay resilient when it feels like the attack might land on any one of our allies or, or us at any moment?

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:14.910
 Do you know what I mean?

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:20.580
 Yeah. Well, I mean, I think that, you know, it's like community has to reinforce that.

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:27.640
 Like we have to sort of feel like, you know, our friends and the people we care about, you know, are engaging in these things.

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:30.970
 And this has to be sort of part of our activities.

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:33.250
 Like it has to, it has to be fun.

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:35.430
 It has to be like, hang out with your friends.

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:35.890
 Right.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:39.570
 Like it doesn't have to be a separate thing.

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:48.350
 And what we need is for our, you know, our social circles to, you know, make that activity part of the socializing.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:55.930
 I mean, you know, it's sort of, it's no secret that a big part of the, you know, success of, of ACT UP was the social aspects of it.

00:32:56.150 --> 00:33:02.670
 And, you know, sort of what a sort of scene developed within the organization of, you know, social scene.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:11.770
 So, you know, people wanted to hang out together and, you know, often it was in the social context and the planning of events and the traveling to events.

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:15.690
 You know, that, that just became our social life.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:16.730
 Right. Yeah.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:26.760
 So my early attempts at making beer, which I like followed all the directions and I like, I made okay beer, but I was always like, this is miserable.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:28.360
 It's just hours in the kitchen.

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:30.780
 And then still someone was like, no, you're doing it wrong.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:33.220
 Like you're supposed to do this with other people.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.300
 You're not supposed to like do it all by yourself.

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:37.840
 Of course, it's miserable to make your own beer that way.

00:33:41.870 --> 00:33:42.270
 Nice.

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:47.350
 And so then the hours in the kitchen, because, you know, it's like this phrase I used to use a lot.

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:55.530
 I'm getting there is half the fun, but like, you know, you just can't think of the time in the kitchen, the time going to a demonstration.

00:33:55.750 --> 00:34:05.730
 And you can't think about that as like, you know, your, your punishment or your chore, you know, you have to, you know, you have to pull a Tom Sawyer.

00:34:05.850 --> 00:34:07.850
 You have to find a way to, to make it fun.

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:08.510
 Yeah, for sure.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:11.010
 How are you doing that now?

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:11.890
 Does that come naturally?

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:13.250
 Yeah, sure.

00:34:13.390 --> 00:34:18.770
 I mean, I, you know, I, I mean, I do a lot of things with people.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:24.190
 I spend so much time with people that actually it feels like a big treat when I'm by myself.

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:29.560
 So, you know, that makes, you know, even the things I do by myself, um, um, feel, feel fun.

00:34:30.390 --> 00:34:30.530
 Great.

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:33.360
 I, you know, it's been a really great conversation, Sander.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:37.980
 Um, is there anything else that you want to talk about that I haven't asked about?

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:39.460
 Nothing specific.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:43.780
 I guess I would mention my website, which is wildfermentation.com.

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:51.260
 And, um, you know, just say that, uh, you know, all of the, uh, uh, workshops that I do, I post on my website.

00:34:51.260 --> 00:35:00.640
 So anyone who's interested in, um, you know, learning, learning more about fermentation, my website's a good place to, um, you know, look for, uh, opportunities for that.

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:13.650
 Um, I have, uh, you've mentioned two of my books, um, the art of fermentation and the revolution will not be microwaved, which is about, uh, basically grassroots movements to, uh, reclaim food.

00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:19.370
 And then I have, uh, um, another book, which is really the book that started it all, which is called wild fermentation.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.890
 And that book was just revised last year.

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:25.630
 So there's a brand new edition of wild fermentation floating around.

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:29.890
 And, um, and mostly I just thank you for having me on your show.

00:35:30.630 --> 00:35:47.920
 And, um, you know, since the, since the theme is, you know, migration, I mean, I'll just say that one of the most, uh, you know, consistently, uh, you know, interesting and exciting interactions that I have about fermentation with people is with immigrants to the, to the United States.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:36:04.460
 And, um, you know, just, I have learned so much about fermentation traditions in different parts of the world, um, from immigrants who have, you know, come to the U S and maybe one of the things that they miss about the place that they left is some of the food culture.

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:17.020
 And, uh, you know, it's just really, um, you know, thrilling, you know, either to be able to just like learn from people who have always been doing this, um, you know, because it's their, um, you know, cultural tradition.

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:29.940
 And they brought it with them or, you know, helping someone who felt like they left those things behind, uh, figure out how they can recreate foods, uh, from the old country that, that they miss.

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:39.980
 So, you know, in terms of migration and people's, um, food memories and associations, um, you know, fermentation is very, very, uh, uh, powerful in that way.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:42.460
 Sure. But wait, wait, give me, give me an example of.

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:44.880
 Oh, I mean, it's just from, from all over.

00:36:44.880 --> 00:37:06.680
 I mean, just a couple of weeks ago, um, uh, I, uh, went into this Asian grocery store in the outskirts of Nashville that was owned by this Burmese family and had, um, you know, such an amazing, uh, interaction with them about, um, uh, fermented Burmese tea leaves, which I, I bought some of them from them.

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:10.380
 Uh, at a conference, uh, at a conference, uh, sustainable agriculture conference.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:12.080
 I was at a couple of years ago.

00:37:12.080 --> 00:37:23.440
 Um, there was a Senegalese fellow who, when I mentioned the name Sambula, which is the Senegalese name for a condiment.

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:28.880
 That's used to very, very widely there that's fermented from African locust beans.

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:42.320
 The guy just like lit up that I had heard of this and he went home and got some of the Sambula that his, uh, you know, someone in his family had sent him from Senegal and, and brought it to me.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:58.630
 So just, you know, experiences like that and, um, and, you know, just because, you know, fermentation is so integral in food traditions in every part of the world, uh, you know, I've had, um, you know, beautiful experiences like this with people from lots of different places.

00:37:58.870 --> 00:37:59.790
 Yeah, no, I bet.

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:00.310
 All right.

00:38:00.350 --> 00:38:01.630
 Well, thanks so much, Sander.

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:04.810
 I really appreciate taking the time and having the conversation.

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:05.730
 Okay.

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:08.210
 Well, thank you so much for having me on your podcast.

